<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ladislaus IV of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1262">1262</a>–<a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ladislaus the Cuman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ladislaus IV, called the Cuman after his mother's people, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1272 to 1290. Crowned as a child, he struggled with baronial factions throughout his reign. His cavalry helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> on the Marchfeld in 1278, and he repelled a second Mongol invasion in 1285–1286, but his favour to the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> brought papal hostility. He was murdered in 1290.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ladislaus-iv-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Ladislaus IV of Hungary" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Szilágyi Sándor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ladislav4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Afterwards Béla IV encouraged stone castles, which helped repel a second Mongol invasion in 1285–1286, and in 1278 <b>Ladislaus IV</b>'s cavalry helped Rudolf of Habsburg defeat Ottokar II of Bohemia on the Marchfeld.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first written evidence of local Vlach voivodes concerns Litovoi, mentioned in the Diploma of the Joannites in 1247 as ruler of Țara Litua in what is now northern Oltenia; he refused tribute to <b>Ladislaus IV of Hungary</b> and died in battle before 1280.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen, the German king Rudolf I of Habsburg, allied with King <b>Ladislaus IV of Hungary</b>, defeated and killed Ottokar II of Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Through his wife Catherine, daughter of Stephen V of Hungary, he received from <b>Ladislaus IV</b> in 1284 the Banate of Macsó, Belgrade, Usora and Soli.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-syrmia">Kingdom of Syrmia</a></li><li><blockquote>A royal charter of 1285 says that around 1277–1279 a voivode Litovoi, with his brothers, seized part of the kingdom and refused to pay tribute to <b>Ladislaus IV of Hungary</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/voivodeship-of-litovoi">Voivodeship of Litovoi</a></li></ul></section>
